    "title": "Redox Biology as an Integrated Network System",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22738348",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "<p>The central thesis of this monograph is that cellular redox biology is<br>most faithfully described as an integrated network system whose state<br>variable is a vector of oxidized-to-reduced ratios&mdash;NAD⁺/NADH,<br>NADP⁺/NADPH, GSH/GSSG, and TrxSS/TrxSH&mdash;resolved by<br>compartment and gated by enzyme kinetics, rather than a scalar index<br>derived from any single couple. Building on a curated synthesis of<br>peer-reviewed primary literature, the work develops the<br>electrochemical and thermodynamic foundations of this view<br>(Nernstian compartmental potentials; non-equilibrium steady states),<br>formalizes the network as a directed graph of enzymatically gated<br>edges, and examines its central metabolic nodes, glutathione and<br>NAD⁺, together with both pathological directions of stress&mdash;oxidative<br>and reductive, the latter mediated by reverse electron transport.<br>Signaling chemistries are treated as network computations:<br>ultrasensitive peroxiredoxin/H₂O₂ kinetics, nuclear sulfenylation,<br>sulfur-based persulfide signaling, and ferroptosis as a redox-dependent<br>cell-fate decision executed by three compartment-mapped removal<br>loops. Physiological integration is traced through circadian&ndash;redox<br>feedback loops, immunometabolic reprogramming, and endoplasmic<br>reticulum redox chemistry. Single-cell heterogeneity is shown to place<br>decisive information in distributions rather than means, motivating the<br>genetically encoded biosensor apparatus summarized in tabular form.<br>Finally, the framework's operational requirements&mdash;ontology-based<br>model interoperability and redox-aware metabolic engineering,<br>exemplified by the Redox Imbalance Forces Drive strategy achieving<br>117.65 g/L L-threonine in engineered Escherichia coli&mdash;are evaluated.<br>The synthesis positions redox biology as a quantitative, systems-level<br>discipline in which measurement, modeling, and design must jointly<br>respect compartmental insulation, bidirectional stress, and<br>out-of-equilibrium dynamics.</p>",
